{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JDNWySmkqMB15ttUTQgSgN","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meta:\\\\n  last_updated: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  total_papers: 10\\\\n\\\\ncategories:\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"gtheory_evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"G-Theory & Evaluation Variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Applying Generalizability Theory and variance decomposition to LLM evaluation reliability\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"infrastructure_variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"Infrastructure-Induced Variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Hardware, precision, and CUDA-level nondeterminism as variance sources in LLM inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"prompt_sensitivity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"Prompt Sensitivity & Methodological Variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Prompt phrasing, seed, temperature, and ordering as variance sources\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"evaluation_methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"Evaluation Methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Scoring artifacts, benchmark design, and evaluation protocol considerations\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\npapers:\\\\n  mesting2026hidden:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Hidden Measurement Error in LLM Pipelines Distorts Annotation, Evaluation, and Benchmarking\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Mesting, F.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11581\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"gtheory_evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Closest methodological precedent: G-theory decomposition of 5 facets (item, prompt, temperature, judge model, replication) on AILuminate/MMLU/Chatbot Arena. Found judge model=43.8%, prompt×judge=12.4%. Critically excludes infrastructure factors — our work extends this by crossing infrastructure with methodology.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  brittlebench2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Brittlebench: Quantifying LLM Robustness via Prompt Sensitivity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.13285\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"prompt_sensitivity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Up to 50% performance variance from prompt perturbations, 63% of cases show ranking reversals from single perturbations. Provides perturbation protocol we follow for prompt paraphrase generation. Does not cross with infrastructure factors.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  yuan2025layercast:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Understanding and Mitigating Numerical Sources of Nondeterminism in LLM Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Yuan, Z.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09501\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"infrastructure_variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Documents up to 9% accuracy variation and 9,000-token response-length shifts from GPU count, type, and precision changes. Proposes LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) mitigation. Does not conduct factorial attribution across multiple variance sources.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  messina2026background:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Introducing Background Temperature to Characterise Hidden Randomness in LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Messina, P.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Scotta, A.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.22411\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"infrastructure_variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Formalizes hidden nondeterminism as effective background temperature T_bg persisting even at nominal T=0 due to floating-point non-associativity in BF16/FP16. Provides theoretical framework for understanding infrastructure-level stochasticity we measure.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  generalizit2024:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"GeneralizIT: A Python Solution for Generalizability Theory Computations\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17880\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"gtheory_evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Python implementation of G-theory computations including D-study projections. Core analysis tool for our variance decomposition pipeline.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  hua2025flaw:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Flaw or Artifact? Rethinking Prompt Sensitivity in Evaluating LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Hua, W.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"evaluation_methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Demonstrates log-likelihood-based evaluation inflates apparent prompt sensitivity — a confounder our generation-based evaluation protocol controls for. Motivates our choice of generation-based scoring.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  thinkingmachines2025batch:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Batch Invariance, Seed Locking, and Beyond: Countering Nondeterminism in LLM Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Technical Report\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"infrastructure_variance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Shows 80/1000 unique completions for Qwen3-8B at T=0 under default vLLM settings. Demonstrates batch-invariant GEMM/RMSNorm/attention kernels eliminate nondeterminism with 1.6-2.1x latency overhead. We use DEFAULT settings (without these kernels) to reflect practitioner conditions.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  kunievsky2025intent:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Measuring Intent Comprehension in LLMs: A Variance Decomposition Framework\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Kunievsky, D.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Evans, J.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16584\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"gtheory_evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Decomposes variance into intent, articulation, and model uncertainty facets. Does not include infrastructure factors. Provides alternative facet structure we compare against.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  bui2025seeds:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Assessing the Macro and Micro Effects of Random Seeds on Fine-Tuning Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Bui, T.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"prompt_sensitivity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Documents extreme seed sensitivity (RTES variance=18.22) in fine-tuning. Motivates our fine-tuning G-study extension. Isolated factor study without infrastructure crossing.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  sameprompt2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Same Prompt, Different Outcomes: Evaluating the Reproducibility of Data Analysis by LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14349\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"prompt_sensitivity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Evaluated 2 strategies x 6 models x 4 temperatures with 10 repeats but excluded hardware/precision. Confirms methodology-layer variance without infrastructure crossing.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T14:00:00Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 6732, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XRzAVWoX691HBWCqVQkXHZ","session_id":"5170c10d-5bd8-4681-9e58-19a837bd2547","uuid":"09a28ac4-d298-46a1-a769-b89e8c7a9ef9","timestamp":"2026-05-20T23:48:21.965Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QnQ7ZxBVMZ4qcNLf6ZaByr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014CMeSdtVHBo4Q54wrKrgBn","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **novelty, significance, positioning against related work, and writing quality**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Variance Decomposition Is a Measurement, Not a Fact: On the Instability of Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation\"\n\nThe paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation variance across 5 benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, MATH), 4 models (Qwen3-8B, Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B), crossing infrastructure (precision, hardware, ordering) × methodology (prompt, seed, temperature) factors.\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. Item dominance estimates are sample-unstable (CV=33% across 3 item resamples). Single variance decompositions over-commit to attribution certainty.\n2. Model×item interaction (33%) is the largest overlooked component; treating model as fixed inflates item variance by >22pp.\n3. D-study reproducibility budgets converge across benchmarks (range=60 items) but carry ±190-item CIs, rendering benchmark-specific recommendations meaningless.\n\n**The paper frames itself as a meta-methodological critique:** variance decomposition is a measurement, not a fact. Prior work (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026, Bouthillier et al. 2021) reports point estimates of variance shares as findings without testing stability.\n\n**Related work cited:**\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021): ANOVA-based variance decomposition for ML benchmarks\n- Henderson et al. (2018): Implementation details in deep RL\n- Dodge et al. (2020), Sellam et al. (2022): Fine-tuning instability\n- Mesting (2026): G-theory for LLM-as-judge evaluation (single benchmark, model-as-fixed, no resampling stability)\n- Brittlebench (2026): Prompt sensitivity up to 50%, fixed hardware\n- Yuan et al. (2025): Up to 9% accuracy variation from hardware/precision\n- Lu et al. (2022), Pezeshkpour & Hruschka (2024): Option ordering effects\n- Biderman et al. (2024): Evaluation reproducibility survey\n- IRT work: Lalor et al. (2019), Rodriguez et al. (2021), Vania et al. (2021), Polo et al. (2024), Perlitz et al. (2023), Dehghani et al. (2021)\n\n**8 experiments:** Full-scale precision study, item resampling stability (centerpiece), multi-benchmark replication, multi-hardware G-study, comprehensive reanalysis (model-as-facet + bootstrap + D-study), 14B extension, fine-tuning G-study, LayerCast mitigation, MATH extension.\n\n**Key narrative arc:** The paper started with a \"cross-benchmark variance gradient\" hypothesis (knowledge→reasoning benchmarks would show decreasing item dominance). This failed (spread=9pp, non-monotonic). The pivot: \"We set out to build reproducibility budgets and discovered the budget itself is unreliable.\"\n\n**Writing structure:** 13 pages. Abstract → Introduction → Related Work → Method (G-theory framework + experimental design) → Experiments and Results (8 subsections) → Discussion → Conclusion. 18 tables, described figure slots but unclear if figures are actually included.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**:\n   - Is the \"variance decomposition is a measurement\" framing genuinely novel, or is this well-known in psychometrics/measurement theory?\n   - The model-as-facet vs model-as-fixed comparison — is this a novel insight for LLM evaluation, or standard G-theory practice?\n   - Item resampling instability — has this been tested before in ML evaluation contexts?\n   - The bimodality→item_id correlation (r=0.95) — is this a meaningful contribution?\n\n2. **Positioning Against Related Work**:\n   - Is the critique of Mesting (2026), Brittlebench (2026) fair and well-substantiated?\n   - Are there missing related works? (e.g., IRT-based item selection literature, sampling theory, measurement error in social sciences)\n   - Does the paper adequately distinguish from Bouthillier et al. (2021)?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact**:\n   - Would these findings change evaluation practice?\n   - Is the \"budgets need error bars\" recommendation actionable?\n   - Does the paper provide solutions or only diagnosis?\n\n4. **Writing Quality**:\n   - Is the narrative pivot (from gradient to instability) handled well or does it feel like post-hoc rationalization?\n   - Is the paper too long? 8 experiments across 13 pages — is there bloat?\n   - Are Experiments 5, 7 contributing enough to justify their space?\n\n5. **Framing Concerns**:\n   - The paper's central message is essentially \"measurement error exists in variance decomposition.\" Is this surprising enough for a top venue?\n   - Does the paper overclaim? (e.g., \"all prior work\" over-commits)\n\n6. **Strengths and Weaknesses** (bulleted)\n\n7. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n\n8. **Score** with justification\n\n9. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nFormat your output as a structured review.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16644,"cache_read_input_tokens":40707,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16644},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5170c10d-5bd8-4681-9e58-19a837bd2547","uuid":"c50cbc4a-7b51-4e8f-b336-7d1c2421de6a"}
