{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UGEHkHEo6PZ42JDtEqahVw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HHKxDfT4eWr5KtJMQXDDYK","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Review Report — Reviewer R2 (Novelty, Significance, Positioning, Writing)\n\n**Paper:** \"Variance Decomposition Is a Measurement, Not a Fact: On the Instability of Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation\"\n\n**Tier:** worst\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n### 1a. \"Variance decomposition is a measurement\" framing\n\nThis framing is **not novel in psychometrics or the broader measurement sciences**. The instability of variance component estimates under resampling is a well-known phenomenon in G-theory. Brennan (2001, Ch. 8) explicitly discusses sampling variability of variance components and recommends bootstrap or jackknife standard errors. Shavelson & Webb (1991) note that component estimates depend on facet sample size and the specific levels sampled. The educational measurement literature has used resampling stability checks for decades (e.g., Cardinet, Johnson, & Pini, 2010). The paper presents this as a discovery (\"we set out to build budgets and discovered the budget is unreliable\"), but anyone trained in G-theory would expect variance component estimates to have sampling distributions. The CV=33% finding is noteworthy in the LLM context, but the epistemological claim is standard measurement theory repackaged as a revelation.\n\n**Verdict:** The framing is novel *for the ML audience* but risks appearing naive to measurement-literate reviewers. The contribution is empirical (documenting the magnitude of instability in LLM evaluation), not conceptual.\n\n### 1b. Model-as-facet vs. model-as-fixed\n\nThis is **standard G-theory practice**, not a novel insight. The distinction between random and fixed facets is one of the first topics covered in any G-theory course. Whether to treat a facet as random or fixed depends on whether one wants to generalize beyond the specific levels observed. Mesting (2026) treating model as fixed is a defensible design choice (she studies a specific set of models), not an error. The paper's claim that prior work \"inflates\" item variance by treating model as fixed conflates a design decision with a methodological flaw. The 22pp shift is informative but expected: adding any large facet as random will redistribute variance from existing components.\n\n**Verdict:** The finding quantifies a known effect in a new domain. Useful, but cannot be positioned as a methodological contribution.\n\n### 1c. Item resampling instability\n\nThis is the paper's strongest novelty claim. To my knowledge, **no prior ML evaluation study has explicitly tested the resampling stability of variance components**. Bouthillier et al. (2021) and Mesting (2026) report point estimates without stability analysis. The demonstration that CV=33% with rank inversions across item samples is a genuinely useful empirical contribution. However, the magnitude of the CV depends heavily on design specifics (200 items from a 14,000-item pool with heterogeneous subject difficulty is expected to produce high variability). With 3 resamples, the CV estimate itself is poorly estimated (the standard error of a CV from n=3 is enormous). The paper needs 10+ resamples to make the CV=33% claim credible.\n\n**Verdict:** Novel for ML evaluation. Underpowered (only 3 resamples to estimate a CV). The contribution is real but the specific numbers are unreliable.\n\n### 1d. Bimodality-item_id correlation (r=0.95)\n\nThis is **potentially interesting but mechanistically trivial** and computed from only 5 data points. An r=0.95 from n=5 has a 95% CI of approximately [0.63, 0.99] under Fisher's z, so it is not as precise as it appears. More importantly, the relationship is tautological at its core: benchmarks with more variable item difficulties (bimodal = some very easy, some very hard) will necessarily produce more item-level variance. This is a mathematical near-identity, not an empirical finding.\n\n**Verdict:** The correlation is real but trivially expected from the definition of item variance. Not a meaningful contribution.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Positioning Against Related Work\n\n### 2a. Critique of Mesting (2026) and Brittlebench (2026)\n\nThe critique is **partially fair but overstated**. The paper claims (lines 43-50) that \"every prior study that reports a single variance decomposition without resampling stability analysis has, in effect, presented one draw from a high-variance distribution as a finding.\" This is true in principle but uncharitable. Mesting's study uses a large item set and reports REML standard errors (which capture estimation uncertainty within a fixed design). Brittlebench uses a different framework entirely (not G-theory). The paper implies these studies are fundamentally flawed rather than acknowledging that they answer different questions with different assumptions.\n\nThe claim that \"if Mesting had used different items, her 43.8% judge-dominance estimate might have been 30% or 55%\" (line 245) is speculative. Mesting's study uses different benchmarks with different item pool sizes; the CV from MMLU does not transfer directly.\n\n### 2b. Missing related work\n\nSeveral important references are absent:\n\n- **Cardinet, Johnson, & Pini (2010)**: \"Applying Generalizability Theory using EduG\" — directly addresses variance component stability and CIs in G-theory\n- **Webb, Shavelson, & Haertel (2006)**: Reliability coefficients and G-theory, including sampling variability of components\n- **Marcoulides (1999)**: On the sampling variability of G-theory estimates\n- **Multiverse analysis literature** (Steegen et al., 2016): The \"analyst degrees of freedom\" argument has been made more rigorously in the multiverse framework\n- **Differential item functioning (DIF) literature**: The model×item interaction is essentially DIF — items that function differently across subpopulations (here, models). This connection to the psychometric DIF literature is completely unmade.\n- **Bell (2010)** and other G-theory sampling papers in educational measurement\n\nThe paper cites Brennan and Shavelson/Webb as canonical references but does not engage with the substantial G-theory literature on exactly the problem it claims to discover (component sampling variability).\n\n### 2c. Distinction from Bouthillier et al. (2021)\n\nAdequately distinguished. The paper correctly notes that Bouthillier uses ANOVA without D-study projections. The distinction is clear.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Significance and Impact\n\n### 3a. Would these findings change evaluation practice?\n\n**Partially.** The resampling instability finding (Claim 1) should prompt researchers to report bootstrap CIs on variance components — this is a useful methodological recommendation. The model-as-facet finding (Claim 2) is relevant but its practical implication is unclear: most evaluation studies compare specific models, making model-as-fixed appropriate by design. The D-study CI finding (Claim 3) is a legitimate concern but the practical response (\"report uncertainty on budgets\") is straightforward.\n\n### 3b. Is \"budgets need error bars\" actionable?\n\n**Yes, but it is a modest recommendation.** The paper essentially says: report confidence intervals on your variance component estimates and D-study projections. This is standard statistical practice that has been advocated in psychometrics for decades. The novelty is in showing that the ML community has not been doing it, which is valuable but incremental.\n\n### 3c. Solutions vs. diagnosis\n\n**The paper is almost entirely diagnostic.** It identifies problems (instability, model specification sensitivity, CI width) but provides no solutions. It does not propose better estimation methods, more stable designs, or alternative frameworks. The three recommendations in the conclusion (report CIs, use model-as-facet, report uncertainty on budgets) are methodological hygiene points, not solutions to the underlying instability.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Writing Quality\n\n### 4a. Narrative pivot\n\nThe pivot from \"we set out to build budgets\" to \"the budget itself is unreliable\" is **well-executed rhetorically** but raises credibility concerns. Was this genuinely a surprise discovery, or was the \"failed hypothesis\" narrative constructed post-hoc to make negative results publishable? The paper framing document reveals this was indeed a pivot after the cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis failed. The pivot is honest, but the resulting narrative feels contrived — the paper is essentially a collection of negative/null results (no gradient, unstable components, wide CIs) reframed as meta-methodological insights.\n\n### 4b. Paper length and bloat\n\n**The paper is too long for its content density.** 8 experiments across what would be 13+ pages is excessive. Specific concerns:\n\n- **Experiment 5 (14B extension)**: Adds very little. With n=2 models and scale/architecture confounded, the finding that item dominance is \"lower for 14B\" is uninterpretable. This should be cut or relegated to a 2-line appendix mention.\n- **Experiment 7 (LayerCast)**: Confirms precision is not a first-order concern — a finding already established in Experiment 1 after confound correction. Adds <2% variance. Should be cut.\n- **Experiment 6 (fine-tuning)**: Uses RoBERTa-large, a fundamentally different setting from LLM inference evaluation. The finding that learning rate dominates (instead of item) in fine-tuning is unsurprising and disconnected from the main narrative. The low correlation between inference and fine-tuning variance structures (r=0.18) is the only noteworthy point, and it could be a 3-sentence appendix finding.\n\nThe paper would be stronger at 8-9 pages with Experiments 1, 1b, 2, 3, and 4 only.\n\n### 4c. Table density\n\n**18 tables with no figures** is a severe presentation problem. The paper violates its own field's conventions (distilled writing patterns call for stacked-bar decomposition figures as the \"signature visual\"). The absence of any figure makes the paper visually impenetrable and prevents readers from grasping patterns at a glance. At minimum, the paper needs: (1) a stacked-bar variance decomposition across benchmarks, (2) a D-study projection curve, and (3) a resampling stability visualization.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Framing Concerns\n\n### 5a. Is \"measurement error exists in variance decomposition\" surprising enough?\n\n**No, not for a top venue.** This is a well-known concern in psychometrics. The contribution is showing its magnitude in LLM evaluation, which is useful but incremental. The paper would need to either (a) show that the instability is catastrophically worse than expected from theory, or (b) propose a solution that substantially improves practice. It does neither.\n\n### 5b. Does the paper overclaim?\n\n**Yes, in several places:**\n\n- \"Every prior study that reports a single variance decomposition...has presented one draw from a high-variance distribution as a finding\" — this over-generalizes from MMLU to all measurement settings\n- \"Model×item interaction (33%) is the single largest overlooked component\" — whether it is \"overlooked\" depends on the research question; treating model as fixed is a legitimate design choice when comparing specific models\n- \"Rendering benchmark-specific budget recommendations statistically meaningless\" — the CIs are wide but the point estimates are still informative; \"meaningless\" is too strong\n- The title \"Variance Decomposition Is a Measurement, Not a Fact\" implies prior work treated decompositions as facts — most measurement researchers know they are estimates\n\n---\n\n## 6. Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1.** The resampling instability analysis (Exp 1b) is a genuinely novel contribution to ML evaluation methodology, even if the concept is known in psychometrics\n- **S2.** Large scale: 4 models, 5 benchmarks, multiple facets crossed — the most comprehensive G-theory study of LLM evaluation to date\n- **S3.** The engine confound discovery and correction (V0 enforce_eager) is a valuable methodological contribution that benefits the community\n- **S4.** The model-as-facet reanalysis, while methodologically standard, provides useful quantification of model×item interaction magnitude in LLM evaluation\n- **S5.** The bimodality mechanism linking difficulty distribution shape to item dominance is intuitive and potentially useful for benchmark designers\n- **S6.** The REML vs. Henderson comparison adds a useful sensitivity dimension\n- **S7.** The pivot narrative is intellectually honest — admitting the gradient hypothesis failed\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1.** The central framing claim (\"variance decomposition is a measurement, not a fact\") is well-established in psychometrics; presenting it as a discovery undermines credibility with measurement-literate reviewers\n- **W2.** Only 3 item resamples to estimate CV — the CV=33% figure is itself highly uncertain; need 10+ resamples for credible estimation\n- **W3.** No figures at all — 18 tables with zero visualizations makes the paper nearly unreadable\n- **W4.** Experiments 5, 6, and 7 contribute little and bloat the paper; the fine-tuning study (RoBERTa) is too far from the LLM inference setting\n- **W5.** The paper is purely diagnostic — no solutions, no improved methods, no practical tools beyond \"report CIs\" (standard advice)\n- **W6.** The critique of prior work is uncharitable: treating model as fixed is a design choice, not an error; Mesting reports REML SEs\n- **W7.** The non-gradient result (Exp 2, 8) is presented as meaningful, but 9pp spread across 5 benchmarks under model-as-fixed is expected given resampling instability — this is a null result, not a finding\n- **W8.** The bimodality correlation (r=0.95 from n=5) is both statistically underpowered and mechanistically tautological\n- **W9.** Missing engagement with the substantial psychometric literature on variance component sampling variability (Cardinet et al., Marcoulides, Webb et al.)\n- **W10.** The paper's three claims are essentially three ways of saying \"variance estimates are uncertain\" — this is one finding, not three\n\n---\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On resampling stability**: With only 3 item resamples, your CV=33% estimate has enormous uncertainty. What is the bootstrap standard error of the CV itself? Have you considered running 30+ resamples to produce a credible estimate? Would the CV decrease with larger item samples (e.g., 500 items from the 14,000-item MMLU pool)?\n\n2. **On model-as-facet**: You argue that treating model as fixed \"inflates\" item variance. But if a practitioner wants to evaluate a specific model (the common use case), treating model as fixed is correct by design — the goal is not to generalize to unseen models. Under what practical scenario is model-as-random the appropriate specification? How do you respond to the argument that model×item interaction is simply model-specific item difficulty, which is exactly what practitioners care about?\n\n3. **On the non-gradient**: Your original hypothesis was a cross-benchmark gradient from knowledge to reasoning. This failed (spread=9pp, non-monotonic). You then pivoted to \"the budget itself is unreliable.\" If the gradient had been confirmed, would you still have discovered the instability? Does the pivot undermine the claim that instability is the \"centerpiece\" finding, or was it only noticed because the primary hypothesis failed?\n\n4. **On separability**: You report that separability fails on GSM8K (4.5% infra×method) and with hardware (5.5%). But 4-5% is arguably still small in absolute terms. What threshold makes separability \"fail\"? Is there a principled way to set this threshold, or is the \"failure\" framing a judgment call?\n\n5. **On engagement with psychometrics**: Your central claim — that variance components are estimates with sampling distributions — is standard in the G-theory literature (Brennan 2001 Ch.8, Cardinet et al. 2010). Why does the paper not engage with this prior work? How does the observed CV=33% compare to typical component instability in educational measurement applications of G-theory?\n\n---\n\n## 8. Score\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a relevant question (stability of variance decomposition in LLM evaluation) and provides useful empirical data at scale. However, the central conceptual contribution — that variance components are estimates with error — is well-established in psychometrics and cannot be claimed as novel. The paper is purely diagnostic with no solutions beyond standard statistical hygiene recommendations. The resampling analysis (the strongest contribution) is underpowered at n=3 samples. The presentation is severely hampered by 18 tables and zero figures. Experiments 5-7 add bulk without proportional insight. The framing overreaches: treating model-as-fixed as an \"error\" rather than a design choice, claiming prior work treats decompositions as \"facts,\" and declaring benchmark-specific budgets \"meaningless.\" For a top venue with ~25% acceptance, the paper needs either a substantially novel conceptual contribution or a practical solution, and it provides neither. The empirical findings (resampling CV, model×item magnitude) are useful contributions that would strengthen a paper with a more modest framing, but the current framing invites expert pushback.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n\nI have substantial familiarity with both G-theory (through educational measurement applications) and LLM evaluation methodology. I have read the primary references (Brennan 2001, Shavelson & Webb 1991, Bouthillier et al. 2021) and am familiar with the variance component sampling variability literature. I am less certain about the specific numerical claims (I cannot verify the 4M observations or the exact variance decomposition numbers), but the methodological assessment is on firm ground.\n","project":"nlp_variance_gtheory","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260520T222113","overall_score":"3 — Weak Reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":728,"cache_read_input_tokens":73542,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":728,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014CMeSdtVHBo4Q54wrKrgBn","session_id":"5170c10d-5bd8-4681-9e58-19a837bd2547","uuid":"c0519999-fc73-43cd-91df-01f8ab7c35df"}
